## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates knowledge of practical data-structure trade-offs for dynamic ranking. Candidates should justify when to sort on demand vs. maintain auxiliary structures.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) key access
- On-demand sorting for top-k retrieval
- Trade-off: simpler updates vs. slower top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class CounterTopK {
  constructor() {
    this.counts = new Map(); // key -> count
  }
  add(key, delta) {
    const cur = this.counts.get(key) || 0;
    const next = cur + delta;
    if (next <= 0) this.counts.delete(key); else this.counts.set(key, next);
  }
  get(key) { return this.counts.get(key) || 0; }
  topK(k) {
    const arr = Array.from(this.counts.entries());
    arr.sort((a,b)=> b[1]-a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return arr.slice(0,k).map(([key]) => key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical DS reasoning for streams where both max and total are needed efficiently, using a familiar two-stack queue pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Two-stack queue with per-element max
- Running sum maintenance for O(1) sum queries
- Amortized analysis of transfer between stacks

## Code Example
```javascript
class MaxQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.in = [];
    this.out = [];
    this.total = 0;
  }
  push(x) {
    const newMax = this.in.length === 0 ? x : Math.max(x, this.in[this.in.length - 1].max);
    this.in.push({ val: x, max: newMax });
    this.total += x;
  }
  pop() {
    if (this.out.length === 0) {
      while (this.in.length > 0) {
        const v = this.in.pop().val;
        const newMax = this.out.length === 0 ? v : Math.max(v, this.out[this.out.length - 1].max);
        this.out.push({ val: v, max: newMax });
      }
    }
    if (this.out.length === 0) return undefined;
    const popped = this.out.pop();
    this.total -= popped.val;
    return popped.val;
  }
  max() {
    const maxIn = this.in.length === 0 ? -Infinity : this.in[this.in.length - 1].max;
    const maxOut = this.out.length === 0 ? -Infinity : this.out[this.out.length - 1].max;
    if (maxIn === -Infinity && maxOut === -Infinity) return undefined;
    return Math.max(maxIn, maxOut);
  }
  sum() {
    return this.total;
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a sliding window of last N elements for max and sum?
- How would you extend to also provide a min() with the same complexity guarantees?

