## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a compact prefix-based dictionary with duplicates and fast prefix queries using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node counts; handling duplicates via passCount and endCount.
- Insert/erase adjust counts; erase stops if word not present.
- Prefix query is O(length(prefix)).

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode{ constructor(){ this.next=new Array(26).fill(null); this.pass=0; this.end=0; }}
class Trie{ constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
 insert(w){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[i]) p.next[i]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[i]; p.pass++; } p.end++; }
 erase(w){ if(!this.contains(w)) return; let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; p.pass--; } p.end--; }
 countPrefix(pfx){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of pfx){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; if(!p) return 0; } return p.pass; }
 contains(w){ let p=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const i=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; p=p.next[i]; if(!p) return false; } return p.end>0; }
 }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for Unicode inputs or dynamic alphabets?
- What are the memory implications for large vocabularies with many prefixes?

## Why This Is Asked
- Introduces a practical autocomplete scenario and a foundational DS.
- Emphasizes handling duplicates and memory management.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node pass-through counts; O(length) ops; dynamic memory.
- Duplicate handling via cnt; pruning unused nodes to bound memory.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor(){ this.next = new Map(); this.cnt = 0; }
}
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node = node.next.get(ch); node.cnt++; } }
  erase(word){ let stack=[]; let node=this.root; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return; node = node.next.get(ch); stack.push([node,ch]); } // decrement
    for(let [nd] of stack){ nd.cnt--; }
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.cnt; }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a dynamic interval data structure that handles duplicates, supports point-sum queries, and enables k-th weight selection—all without scanning all intervals. It combines segment trees with per-node multisets to achieve range updates and efficient multi-set queries.

## Key Concepts
- Segment tree over coordinate-compressed points for efficient range queries
- Per-node multiset of (weight, id) pairs to handle duplicate intervals
- nodeSum aggregation for fast sum queries at any point
- Binary search over the weight domain combined with order statistics for k-th largest queries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch for insertInterval/eraseInterval
class IntervalIndex {
  constructor(points) {
    this.tree = new SegmentTree(points.length - 1);
  }

  insertInterval(l, r, w, id) {
    const range = this.getCompressedRange(l, r);
    this.tree.update(range.l, range.r, (node) => {
      node.multiset.add({ weight: w, id });
      node.sum += w;
    });
  }

  eraseInterval(id, w) {
    // Find all intervals containing this id and remove one instance
    this.tree.queryById(id, (node) => {
      node.multiset.delete({ weight: w, id });
      node.sum -= w;
    });
  }

  sumWeightsCoveringPoint(p) {
    const idx = this.compress(p);
    return this.tree.queryPoint(idx, (node) => node.sum);
  }

  kthLargestWeightCoveringPoint(p, k) {
    const idx = this.compress(p);
    const weights = this.tree.getWeightsAt(idx);
    return this.findKthLargest(weights, k);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory for huge coordinate spaces with sparse intervals?
- Can we support range-sum queries (total weight over an interval [L, R]) without modification?
- How does this approach scale with high duplicate counts, and what alternatives exist?

